        <tr><td>Washing machine, dishwasher or ice maker line failure</td><td>Usually covered</td><td class="faint">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water heater tank failure</td><td>Water damage usually covered; the heater itself usually not</td><td>Home warranty, for the appliance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toilet overflow</td><td>Usually covered if sudden</td><td class="faint">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Roof leak after wind, hail or a fallen tree</td><td>Usually covered</td><td class="faint">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Roof leak from age or wear</td><td>Roof repair usually not covered</td><td class="faint">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ice dam leak</td><td>Interior water damage often covered</td><td class="faint">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Slow leak over weeks or months</td><td>Usually not covered</td><td class="faint">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sewer or drain backup</td><td>Not covered without an endorsement</td><td>Water backup endorsement; some city programs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sump pump failure or overflow</td><td>Not covered without an endorsement</td><td>Water backup and sump discharge endorsement</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flood, storm surge or surface water from outside</td><td>Not covered</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Groundwater seeping through the foundation</td><td>Not covered</td><td>Usually nothing; it's treated as maintenance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mold</td><td>Sometimes covered when it follows a covered loss, often capped</td><td>Mold endorsement on some policies</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small faint">General patterns for the common HO-3 form. Policies, endorsements and state rules differ. Your policy language controls.</p>

  <h2>The test insurers apply: sudden and accidental</h2>
  <p>Homeowners policies are written to cover sudden events, not maintenance. When an adjuster looks at water damage, the questions are usually where the water came from, when it started, and whether the homeowner could have known about it and stopped it. A pipe that split during a cold snap is sudden. A shower pan that seeped into the subfloor for a year, leaving rot and staining, is gradual.</p>
  <p>That's why documentation matters. Photos and video of the source, the failed part itself, and a note of when you found the water all help show that the damage was sudden.</p>

  <h2>What the policy pays for, and what it doesn't</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Usually paid:</strong> water extraction and drying, removal and replacement of damaged drywall, flooring, cabinets and trim, and damaged belongings under your personal property coverage.</li>
    <li><strong>Often paid:</strong> the cost of tearing out and replacing part of a wall or floor to reach the broken pipe.</li>
    <li><strong>Usually not paid:</strong> the failed item itself, such as the corroded pipe, the worn-out water heater or the old roof.</li>
    <li><strong>Loss of use:</strong> if a covered loss makes the home unlivable, additional living expenses for a hotel or rental and extra meal costs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Flood insurance is separate</h2>
  <p>Homeowners policies exclude flood: water that rises from outside and covers normally dry land, from rivers, heavy rain, storm surge or overwhelmed storm drains. Flood coverage comes from the National Flood Insurance Program, run by FEMA and sold through insurance agents, or from private flood insurers.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period before coverage starts, so you can't buy one when a storm is already on the way.</li>
    <li>NFIP building coverage goes up to $250,000 and contents up to $100,000 for a home.</li>
    <li>In basements, NFIP coverage is limited to items such as furnaces, water heaters, electrical panels, sump pumps and cleanup. Finished walls, floors and most belongings stored in a basement aren't covered.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sewer backup coverage is an add-on</h2>
  <p>Water that backs up through floor drains, toilets or tubs from a sewer line or a failed sump pump is excluded from most standard policies. A water backup endorsement adds it, usually with its own limit. It's often inexpensive compared with the cost of a sewage cleanup, and worth asking your agent about if you have a basement. Some cities also run backup assistance programs; check before you pay for cleanup.</p>

  <h2>How to protect your claim</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Stop the water and make the home safe.</strong> Policies require you to protect the property from further damage, and the cost of reasonable emergency steps is usually part of the claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Document before cleanup.</strong> Take video and photos of every affected room and the source, and keep the failed part.</li>
    <li><strong>Notify your insurer promptly.</strong> Ask for the claim number, your adjuster's contact details, and whether they need to approve a mitigation company before work starts.</li>
    <li><strong>Start drying.</strong> Waiting for an adjuster before drying can turn a drying job into a mold job. Most insurers expect mitigation to begin right away.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep every receipt and an inventory</strong> of damaged belongings, with photos, ages and approximate values.</li>
    <li><strong>Get decisions in writing.</strong> If the insurer denies part of the claim, ask for the specific policy provision it relies on.</li>
  </ol>

